-- Q1652: Audit Sampling [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The most reliable method available for statistical sampling","Required by professional standards when testing cyclical transactions","Preferred over random selection for sampling large populations","Acceptable only if the auditor can demonstrate blocks are representative"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Block (cluster) selection involves selecting contiguous items, such as all transactions in a specific time period. Under AU-C 530, this method is generally discouraged because a few blocks may not represent the full population. It is acceptable only when the auditor can demonstrate representativeness.
Wrong (A): Block selection is not the most reliable method; random or systematic selection provides better statistical validity.
Wrong (B): No standard requires block selection for cyclical transactions; random selection is typically preferred.
Wrong (C): Block selection is not preferred over random selection regardless of population size.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1652;

-- Q1656: Audit Sampling [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Determine the exact value of the entire population","Calculate the appropriate audit fee for the engagement","Identify each individual misstatement within the population","Provide a range where the true population value likely falls"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): In classical variables sampling under AU-C 530, the confidence interval provides a range within which the true population value is expected to fall at a given confidence level. The auditor compares this range against the recorded amount and tolerable misstatement to assess materiality.
Wrong (A): Sampling estimates a range, not an exact population value; only a complete census could do that.
Wrong (B): The confidence interval relates to population valuation, not audit fee calculation.
Wrong (C): The interval estimates the total population value, not individual item misstatements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1656;

-- Q1657: Audit Sampling [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Stratified sampling with full examination of the top stratum","Block selection applied to the highest dollar items","Haphazard sampling with a materiality threshold applied","Attribute sampling focused on large account balances"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 530, testing all items over a specified threshold (100% of the top stratum) and sampling the remainder is a stratified sampling approach. The large items are examined individually because they present the greatest dollar exposure.
Wrong (B): Block selection involves choosing contiguous items from a sequence, not selecting items by dollar amount.
Wrong (C): Haphazard sampling involves selecting items without a structured method, not examining all items above a threshold.
Wrong (D): Attribute sampling tests rates of deviation in controls, not dollar amounts in substantive testing.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1657;

-- Q1658: Audit Sampling [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The auditor need not define a specific sampling objective beforehand","Results cannot support any conclusions about the full population","The auditor must still consider tolerable rate and expected deviation rate","Nonstatistical sampling always produces a smaller sample than statistical"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 530, nonstatistical sampling requires the auditor to use professional judgment but must still consider the same conceptual factors as statistical sampling: tolerable rate, expected deviation rate, and desired assurance level, even though these are not quantified mathematically.
Wrong (A): A sampling objective must always be defined regardless of the sampling approach used.
Wrong (B): Nonstatistical sampling results can support conclusions about the population when properly designed and evaluated.
Wrong (D): Nonstatistical sampling does not inherently require smaller samples; size depends on the auditor''s judgment of risk factors.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1658;

-- Q1659: Audit Sampling [medium/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The planned reliance on the control is fully supported by results","The sample size selected was too large for this test","Planned reliance is not supported and control risk should increase","The tolerable deviation rate should be reduced and the control retested"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 530, when the achieved upper deviation rate (7%) exceeds the tolerable deviation rate (5%), the sample results do not support planned reliance on the control. The auditor should revise the assessed level of control risk upward and plan additional substantive procedures.
Wrong (A): An upper deviation rate exceeding the tolerable rate means results do not support reliance on the control.
Wrong (B): Sample size is unrelated to whether the deviation rate exceeds the tolerable threshold.
Wrong (D): Reducing the tolerable rate would widen the gap further; the appropriate response is to increase assessed control risk.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1659;

-- Q1662: Audit Sampling [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Required sample size increases as the rate gap narrows","Required sample size decreases due to higher expected deviations","Required sample size remains unchanged at the same confidence level","The auditor should switch to a variables sampling approach"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 530, when the expected population deviation rate increases from 1.5% to 3% while the tolerable rate stays at 6%, the gap between expected and tolerable rates narrows. A larger sample is needed to determine with the same confidence whether the true deviation rate exceeds the tolerable rate.
Wrong (B): A narrower gap requires more evidence, not less, to distinguish expected from tolerable rates.
Wrong (C): Changing the expected deviation rate directly affects sample size calculations; it cannot remain unchanged.
Wrong (D): The type of sampling is determined by the audit objective, not by changes in the expected deviation rate.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1662;

-- Q1673: Substantive Procedures [medium/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Disclaim an opinion because missing the count is always a material limitation","Perform alternative procedures to obtain sufficient evidence about inventory","Accept management''s inventory count without performing further testing procedures","Resign from the engagement due to the inability to observe the count"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 501.05, when the auditor cannot attend the physical inventory count, alternative procedures should be performed. These include observing a subsequent count, testing rollback or rollforward of intervening transactions, and inspecting purchase and sales documentation.
Wrong (A): A disclaimer is premature without first attempting alternative procedures as required by AU-C 501.
Wrong (C): Accepting management''s count without corroboration would not provide sufficient appropriate audit evidence.
Wrong (D): Resignation is not required; alternative procedures may provide sufficient evidence about inventory quantities.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1673;

-- Q1677: Substantive Procedures [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A modest 5% decline in revenue compared to the prior year","A change in depreciation method from straight-line to double-declining","Debt covenant violations combined with inability to obtain additional financing","A minor pending lawsuit with a probable loss estimated at $10,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 570.A3, substantial doubt about going concern is raised by conditions such as recurring operating losses, debt covenant violations, inability to obtain financing, and depletion of working capital. Multiple adverse indicators together strongly suggest the entity may not meet its obligations.
Wrong (A): A modest 5% revenue decline alone does not typically indicate inability to continue as a going concern.
Wrong (B): Changing a depreciation method is an accounting policy choice, not an indicator of financial distress.
Wrong (D): A minor lawsuit with a small probable loss does not raise substantial doubt about the entity''s viability.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1677;

-- Q1680: Substantive Procedures [easy/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Pending or threatened litigation, including likelihood of unfavorable outcome","The entity''s overall financial health and operational condition","The adequacy of the entity''s internal controls over financial reporting","Whether the entity''s tax returns were filed on time with authorities"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 501.12, the inquiry letter to legal counsel specifically requests information about pending or threatened litigation, claims, and assessments. The attorney evaluates the likelihood of an unfavorable outcome and provides an estimate of potential loss consistent with ASC 450.
Wrong (B): The letter does not request evaluation of the entity''s overall financial condition, only specific legal matters.
Wrong (C): Internal control assessment is the auditor''s responsibility, not something requested from legal counsel.
Wrong (D): Tax filing timeliness is outside the scope of the legal inquiry letter under AU-C 501.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1680;

-- Q1684: Substantive Procedures [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Require the client to perform a full physical count at year-end","Accept the perpetual records without performing any further testing","Evaluate the perpetual system reliability and observe selected cycle counts","Disclaim an opinion because no full year-end physical count was performed"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 501.A14, when a client uses perpetual inventory with cycle counts, the auditor evaluates the reliability of the perpetual system, observes selected cycle counts during the year, tests reconciliation between cycle count results and perpetual records, and tests the roll-forward to year-end.
Wrong (A): AU-C 501.12 does not require a full year-end count when the perpetual system is reliable and adequate procedures are performed.
Wrong (B): Accepting perpetual records without testing would leave the existence assertion unsupported.
Wrong (D): A disclaimer is inappropriate when the perpetual system is reliable and alternative procedures are available.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1684;

-- Q1686: Substantive Procedures [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only the client''s trial balance and general ledger detail","The prior year audit report and management representation letter","The client''s marketing materials and advertising disclosures","Proxy statements, SEC filings, organizational charts, and management inquiry"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 550.12, related parties are identified by reviewing proxy statements, SEC filings, organizational charts, and conflict-of-interest questionnaires, and by inquiring of management about relationships with officers, directors, and affiliates under AU-C 550.14.
Wrong (A): The trial balance alone would not reveal related party relationships that are not evident from transaction descriptions.
Wrong (B): The prior year audit report may mention known parties but does not identify new or changed relationships.
Wrong (C): Marketing materials do not contain information about ownership structures or related party affiliations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1686;

-- Q1687: Substantive Procedures [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Whether the audit fee has already been collected from the client","Whether the client specifically requests the auditor to investigate further","Whether the applicable statute of limitations has already expired","Whether the statements were issued and the fact would have affected the report"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 560.14-.17, when the auditor discovers a fact that existed at the report date, the response depends on whether the financial statements have already been issued to third parties and whether the newly discovered fact would have affected the auditor''s report. These two factors determine the required course of action.
Wrong (A): The audit fee collection status has no bearing on the auditor''s professional obligations regarding subsequently discovered facts.
Wrong (B): The auditor''s duty to investigate exists regardless of whether the client makes such a request.
Wrong (C): The statute of limitations is a legal concept unrelated to the auditor''s professional responsibilities under AU-C 560.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1687;

-- Q1692: Substantive Procedures [medium/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Always name the specialist in the unmodified audit report","Require the specialist to sign the audit report as a co-author","Disclaim responsibility for all of the specialist''s conclusions","Not refer to the specialist when issuing an unmodified opinion"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 620.15, when issuing an unmodified opinion, the auditor should not refer to the work of an auditor''s specialist in the report. Such reference might be misunderstood as a qualification or division of responsibility. Reference is permitted only with a modified opinion.
Wrong (A): Naming the specialist in an unmodified report is inappropriate because it could imply shared responsibility for the opinion.
Wrong (B): The specialist does not sign the audit report; the auditor retains sole responsibility for the opinion.
Wrong (C): The auditor cannot disclaim responsibility for the specialist''s work; AU-C 620.14 requires evaluation of the specialist''s findings.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1692;

-- Q1693: Substantive Procedures [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The auditor selects items, sends requests, and receives responses directly","The auditor reviews confirmations only after management sends them out","Management sends confirmations and then provides copies to the auditor","The client''s internal audit department manages the entire confirmation process"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 505.08-.10, maintaining control means the auditor personally determines selection of items, prepares or supervises preparation of requests, sends them directly to confirming parties, and ensures responses are returned directly to the auditor without client interception.
Wrong (B): Reviewing confirmations after management sends them does not constitute maintaining control, as management could alter responses.
Wrong (C): Allowing management to send confirmations compromises evidence integrity because management could intercept or modify them.
Wrong (D): Delegating the process to internal audit does not satisfy the external auditor''s control requirement under AU-C 505.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1693;

-- Q1700: Substantive Procedures [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Review only standard recurring journal entries for completeness purposes","Accept management''s explanation that all journal entries are appropriate","Test non-standard entries, especially those made near period-end or to unusual accounts","Test only journal entries that exceed a specific predetermined dollar threshold"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 240.32, when fraud risk involving journal entries exists, the auditor should test non-standard entries, particularly those made near period-end, posted to unusual account combinations, prepared by senior personnel outside normal duties, or lacking adequate supporting documentation.
Wrong (A): Reviewing only standard entries would miss the non-standard entries most likely to be used for manipulation.
Wrong (B): Accepting management''s explanation without testing would not address the identified fraud risk.
Wrong (D): Using only a dollar threshold could miss entries designed to stay below that threshold to avoid detection.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1700;

-- Q1703: Substantive Procedures [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept the January recording because the invoices were paid on time","Notify the SEC of the late recording of the December invoices","Propose an adjusting entry and evaluate whether the misstatement is material","Ignore the timing difference if amounts fall below the significant threshold"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Invoices for goods or services received before year-end that were recorded in January represent a cutoff error understating accounts payable. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should propose an adjusting entry to record the payables in the proper period and evaluate whether the aggregate misstatement is material.
Wrong (A): Timely payment does not excuse a cutoff error; the liability existed at year-end and should have been recorded then.
Wrong (B): A cutoff error does not trigger an SEC notification obligation; it is addressed through the audit process.
Wrong (D): Cutoff errors must be evaluated in aggregate with other misstatements, not dismissed based on individual item size.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1703;

-- Q1708: Substantive Procedures [medium/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept management''s assertion that the transactions are at arm''s length","Ignore the transactions because spousal relationships are not related parties","Examine transaction terms, compare to market rates, and verify disclosures","Require the CEO to divest the spouse''s ownership interest in the entity"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 550.20, the auditor should evaluate related party transactions by examining terms and conditions, comparing them to arm''s-length transactions, assessing economic substance and business purpose, and verifying that required disclosures are included in the financial statements.
Wrong (A): Accepting management''s assertion without independent evaluation would not provide sufficient audit evidence under AU-C 550.
Wrong (B): Spousal relationships are considered related parties under ASC 850; these transactions cannot be ignored.
Wrong (D): The auditor has no authority to require divestiture; the auditor''s role is to evaluate and ensure proper disclosure.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1708;

-- Q1710: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A statement that the auditor verified all transactions during the period","An addressee identifying the intended recipients of the report","A title that includes the word independent to indicate objectivity","The auditor''s manual or printed signature on the final report"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 700, the auditor''s report does not state that the auditor verified all transactions. That would imply absolute assurance, whereas an audit provides only reasonable assurance. This statement is not a required element.
Wrong (B): The addressee is a required element of the standard unmodified audit report under AU-C 700.
Wrong (C): AU-C 700 requires the report title to include the word "independent" to indicate the auditor''s objectivity.
Wrong (D): The auditor''s signature is a required element of the audit report under AU-C 700.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1710;

-- Q1721: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The specific audit procedures performed and their detailed results","The dollar amount of each misstatement identified during the audit","Why it was considered a key audit matter and how it was addressed","Management''s formal written response to the identified audit risk"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 701.13, for each key audit matter, the auditor must describe why the matter was considered a key audit matter and how the matter was addressed in the audit. This provides transparency about significant audit judgments.
Wrong (A): KAM disclosures do not require detailed results of specific procedures performed during the audit.
Wrong (B): Specific misstatement dollar amounts are not included in the key audit matter section of the report.
Wrong (D): Management''s response to audit risks is not a required element of the KAM disclosure under AU-C 701.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1721;

-- Q1725: Audit Reports [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Withdraw from the engagement immediately without further steps","Issue an adverse opinion on the audited financial statements","Communicate the matter to governance and consider report implications","Take no further action since the other information is unaudited"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 720.12, if the auditor identifies a material inconsistency between other information and the audited financial statements and management refuses to revise, the auditor communicates with those charged with governance. AU-C 720.13 requires considering an other-matter paragraph or, in serious cases, withdrawal.
Wrong (A): Immediate withdrawal without first communicating with governance bypasses required procedures under AU-C 720.
Wrong (B): An adverse opinion relates to the financial statements themselves, not to inconsistencies in other information.
Wrong (D): The auditor has an affirmative responsibility under AU-C 720 to address material inconsistencies in other information.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1725;

-- Q1733: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Immediately issue a disclaimer of opinion on the financial statements","Request removal of the limitation and communicate with governance","Withdraw from the engagement without any further communication required","Issue an unmodified opinion and document the limitation in workpapers"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 705, when management imposes a scope limitation, the auditor should first request removal. If refused, the auditor communicates with those charged with governance and determines whether alternative procedures are possible before deciding on the appropriate opinion modification.
Wrong (A): Issuing a disclaimer is premature before requesting removal of the limitation and exploring alternatives.
Wrong (C): Withdrawal without communication is not the required first step; the auditor must first attempt to resolve the limitation.
Wrong (D): An unmodified opinion is inappropriate when a material scope limitation exists and has not been resolved.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1733;

-- Q1736: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only a general statement that the financial statements are misstated","A recommendation for how management should correct the departure","A reference to the applicable tax code section for the departure","A description of the matter and quantification of effects if practicable"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 705, the Basis for Qualified Opinion section must describe the matter giving rise to the modification. For misstatements, the auditor quantifies the financial effects if practicable. For scope limitations, the auditor describes why sufficient evidence could not be obtained.
Wrong (A): A general statement alone is insufficient; the specific matter and its financial effects must be described.
Wrong (B): The auditor does not make recommendations for corrective action in the Basis for Qualified Opinion section.
Wrong (C): Tax code references are not relevant to the Basis for Qualified Opinion section addressing a GAAP departure.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1736;

-- Q1746: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["International Financial Reporting Standards applicable to the entity","Auditing standards generally accepted in the United States of America","Generally accepted accounting principles used by the reporting entity","Internal auditing standards issued by the Institute of Internal Auditors"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 700, the Basis for Opinion section states the audit was conducted in accordance with auditing standards generally accepted in the United States of America (GAAS). These standards govern how the audit is performed.
Wrong (A): IFRS is a financial reporting framework, not the auditing standard referenced in the Basis for Opinion section.
Wrong (C): GAAP is the framework against which financial statements are evaluated, not the standard governing audit conduct.
Wrong (D): Internal auditing standards govern internal audit functions, not external audits conducted under GAAS.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1746;

-- Q1749: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An unmodified opinion because disclosures are supplementary","An adverse opinion due to the material departure from GAAP","A disclaimer of opinion citing insufficient audit evidence obtained","A qualified opinion for the material but not pervasive omission"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 705, an omitted disclosure required by GAAP constitutes a departure from the applicable financial reporting framework. If the departure is material but not pervasive, a qualified opinion is appropriate with a Basis for Qualified Opinion paragraph describing the omission.
Wrong (A): An unmodified opinion is inappropriate when a material disclosure is omitted, as this is a GAAP departure.
Wrong (B): An adverse opinion is reserved for misstatements or omissions that are both material and pervasive to the financial statements.
Wrong (C): A disclaimer relates to inability to obtain sufficient evidence, not to a known disclosure omission.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1749;

-- Q1750: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Evaluate whether substantial doubt exists about continued going concern status","Guarantee the entity''s ability to continue as a going concern indefinitely","Predict the entity''s future profitability over the next several fiscal years","Determine whether the entity should file for bankruptcy protection immediately"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 570.10, the auditor''s responsibility is to evaluate whether substantial doubt exists about the entity''s ability to continue as a going concern for a reasonable period, defined as one year from the date the financial statements are issued or available to be issued.
Wrong (B): The auditor provides reasonable assurance on financial statements, not a guarantee of the entity''s continued viability.
Wrong (C): Predicting future profitability is a management responsibility and beyond the scope of an audit engagement.
Wrong (D): Determining whether to file for bankruptcy is a management and legal decision, not an auditor obligation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1750;

-- Q1754: Review and Compilation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Comparing recorded amounts to physical counts of the client''s inventory","Evaluating financial information through analysis of plausible relationships","Confirming account balances directly with independent third parties","Testing internal controls over the entity''s financial reporting process"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AR-C 90.24, analytical procedures in a review engagement involve evaluating financial information by studying plausible relationships among both financial and nonfinancial data, including comparisons to prior years, industry data, and expected relationships.
Wrong (A): Physical counts are audit-level procedures under AU-C standards that exceed the scope of a review engagement.
Wrong (C): Confirming balances with third parties is an audit procedure, not required in a review under AR-C 90.
Wrong (D): Testing internal controls is an audit procedure and is beyond the scope of a review engagement under AR-C 90.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1754;

-- Q1755: Review and Compilation [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The accountant must verify the accuracy of underlying accounting records","The accountant must confirm all material account balances with third parties","The accountant must perform analytical procedures on all material accounts","The accountant should read the statements and consider them for obvious errors"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AR-C 80.17, in a compilation the accountant reads the financial statements and considers whether they appear appropriate in form and free from obvious material misstatements. No assurance is provided and no verification is required.
Wrong (A): Verifying accounting records exceeds the scope of a compilation and would constitute audit-level work.
Wrong (B): Confirming balances with third parties is an audit procedure not required in a compilation under AR-C 80.
Wrong (C): Analytical procedures are required in review engagements under AR-C 90, not in compilations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1755;

-- Q1757: Review and Compilation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Prepares financial statements without being required to issue a report","Must issue a report that expresses limited assurance on the statements","Must be independent of the entity before accepting the engagement","Must verify the accuracy of the trial balance before preparing statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AR-C 70, the preparation engagement involves the accountant assisting management in preparing financial statements without issuing a report and without providing any assurance. Independence is not required for preparation engagements.
Wrong (B): Limited assurance reports are issued in review engagements under AR-C 90, not preparation engagements.
Wrong (C): Independence is not required for preparation engagements under AR-C 70; it is required for reviews under AR-C 90.
Wrong (D): The accountant is not required to verify the trial balance in a preparation engagement under AR-C 70.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1757;

-- Q1758: Review and Compilation [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Yes, independence is always required for compilation engagements","Yes, unless the entity qualifies as a nonprofit organization","No, independence is never relevant for compilation engagement reports","No, but the lack of independence must be disclosed in the report"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AR-C 80.24, independence is not required for compilation engagements. However, if the accountant is not independent, the compilation report must include a statement indicating the lack of independence. The reason for impairment need not be disclosed.
Wrong (A): Independence is required for review engagements under AR-C 90.21, but not for compilations under AR-C 80.
Wrong (B): The nonprofit status of an entity has no bearing on the independence requirements for compilation engagements.
Wrong (C): Independence is relevant because the accountant must disclose the lack of independence if it exists.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1758;

-- Q1763: Review and Compilation [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["We have audited the accompanying financial statements of the entity","We have verified all transactions and account balances during our engagement","The financial statements are guaranteed to be free from material misstatement","We are not aware of material modifications that should be made to the statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AR-C 90, the standard review report includes the limited assurance conclusion: the accountant is not aware of any material modifications that should be made to the financial statements for them to be in accordance with the applicable framework. This negative assurance is the hallmark of a review.
Wrong (A): "We have audited" is audit report language under AU-C 700, not review report language under AR-C 90.
Wrong (B): Verification of all transactions implies absolute assurance, which no engagement type provides.
Wrong (C): No engagement provides a guarantee of freedom from material misstatement; reviews provide only limited assurance.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1763;
